+### Subtopics
+*   **Deferred Surface Splatting:** Computes surface normals for splat projection [G]. Differs by explicitly handling surface orientation, whereas this method uses an affine approximation to projective mapping for perspective correctness without explicit normals [G]. [Link](1072-deferred-surface-splatting.md)
+*   **Opacity-Thin-Side (OTS):** Adjusts opacity based on viewing angle for thin surfaces [G]. Differs by focusing on opacity adjustment for thin geometry, while this method focuses on perspective-correct shape and EWA filtering [G]. [Link](1155-opacity-thin-side-ots.md)
+*   **OTS (EWA-Based Splatting):** Combines OTS with EWA filtering [G]. Differs by integrating opacity thin-side logic with the EWA filter, whereas this method uses a simpler affine approximation for perspective correction [G]. [Link](1181-ots-ewa-based-splatting.md)
+*   **GPU-Accelerated Volume Splatting With Elliptical RBFs:** Uses elliptical Radial Basis Functions for volume rendering [G]. Differs by using elliptical RBFs for volume data, while this method is for point-sampled geometry with an affine EWA approximation [G]. [Link](1186-gpu-accelerated-volume-splatting-with-elliptical-rbfs.md)
+*   **Ellipsoidal Kernel Splatting with Warped Slicing:** Uses warped slicing to handle ellipsoidal kernels [G]. Differs by using a slicing-based approach for ellipsoidal kernels, whereas this method uses direct splat rasterization with an affine approximation [G]. [Link](1290-ellipsoidal-kernel-splatting-with-warped-slicing.md)
+*   **Adaptive EWA Volume Splatting:** Adapts the EWA filter based on local density [G]. Differs by adapting the filter size, while this method uses a fixed affine approximation for perspective correctness [G]. [Link](2137-adaptive-ewa-volume-splatting.md)
+*   **EWA Approximation with Tangent Vector Adjustment:** Adjusts tangent vectors to improve EWA approximation [G]. Differs by adjusting tangent vectors, whereas this method uses an affine approximation to the projective mapping [G]. [Link](2143-ewa-approximation-with-tangent-vector-adjustment.md)
+*   **Phong Splatting:** Applies Phong shading to splats [G]. Differs by adding shading, while this method focuses on perspective-correct rasterization and EWA filtering [G]. [Link](2802-phong-splatting.md)
+*   **EWA Splatting Renderer (Mesh-Free):** A general EWA splatting renderer for mesh-free data [G]. Differs by being a general renderer, while this method specifically uses an affine approximation for perspective correctness [G]. [Link](4665-ewa-splatting-renderer-mesh-free.md)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The vertex program is relatively simple, while the fragment program is more complex due to the EWA filter evaluation. Compared to raycasting, the splatting approach requires simpler vertex programs but significantly more complex fragment programs [S1]. However, since the number of rasterized fragments is significantly larger than the number of splats (at least a factor of four in complete minification), the splatting approach is always more performant [S1].
